\newcommand{\egr}{\operatorname{egr}}
\title{Cubic graphs from complete catalogues}
\author{Human Compression project}
\date{13 October 2026}
\begin{document}\maketitle
An $\egr(n,3,g,\lambda)$ is a connected simple cubic graph on $n$
vertices, of girth $g$, with exactly $\lambda$ unoriented girth cycles
through each edge. Write $n(3,g,\lambda)$ for its minimum possible
order. We recover eight exact extremal counts and eight finite lower
bounds in Table~1 of the fixed June 2024 manuscript of Goedgebeur and
Jooken~\cite{F012}. The results remain attributed to that manuscript
and the earlier work specified there. Our proof applies one exact
cycle test to complete cubic catalogues. Catalogue completeness is
an explicitly retained classification premise; neither the number of
input lines nor the successful execution of a generator proves it.

\begin{theorem}\label{main}
The following minima and numbers of extremal isomorphism classes hold:
\[
\begin{array}{rr|rr}
g&\lambda&n(3,g,\lambda)&\text{classes}\\\hline
5&2&20&1\\
6&2&24&2\\
6&4&18&1\\
6&6&16&1\\
6&8&14&1\\
7&4&28&1\\
8&8&40&1\\
8&16&30&1
\end{array}
\]
In addition,
\[
\begin{array}{rr|r}
g&\lambda&n(3,g,\lambda)\text{ is at least}\\\hline
6&2&24\\
7&2&42\\
7&6&42\\
8&4&48\\
8&6&48\\
8&8&40\\
8&10&48\\
8&12&48
\end{array}
\]
\end{theorem}
The two tables overlap at two minima: their entries are different
obligations in the source audit, not sixteen different mathematical
theorems. In particular the result at $(3,8,8)$ is the previously
known counterexample discussed in Section~4 of~\cite{F012}; it is not
claimed here as a new solution of a conjecture.

\section{Admissible orders}
Counting edges and then edge--cycle incidences gives
\begin{equation}\label{div}
             2\mid n,\qquad 2g\mid3n\lambda.
\end{equation}
Indeed $|E|=3n/2$, and each girth cycle contributes $g$ incidences,
whereas each edge contributes $\lambda$. These elementary conditions
are attributed to Jajcay, Kiss and Miklavi\v c in the source.

We acquire precisely the tree-bound argument in the accepted note
\emph{Lower bounds from short cycles}~\cite{lower}, which in turn
acquires the actual proof of the lemma labelled \texttt{bounds} in
our L025 recovery. That proof gives
\[
 B(3,2s+1,\lambda)=1+3\sum_{i=0}^{s-1}2^i+2^s-\lambda,
 \quad
 B(3,2s,\lambda)=2\sum_{i=0}^{s-1}2^i+
                   \left\lceil\frac{2(2^s-\lambda)}3\right\rceil.
\]
The order is at least $B$. This is the bound of Drglin, Filipovski,
Jajcay and Raiman used by the source. No later L025 assertion that
cites F012 is acquired. Let $b$ be the first integer at least $B$
satisfying~\eqref{div}. The modulus in the table below is
$\operatorname{lcm}(2,2g/\gcd(3\lambda,2g))$.
\begin{center}\begin{tabular}{rr|rrl|r}
\toprule
$g$&$\lambda$&$b$&modulus&orders excluded here&new bound\\\midrule
6&2&18&2&18,20,22&24\\
7&2&28&14&28&42\\
7&6&28&14&28&42\\
8&4&40&4&40,44&48\\
8&6&40&8&40&48\\
8&8&36&2&36,38&40\\
8&10&40&8&40&48\\
8&12&36&4&36,40,44&48\\\bottomrule
\end{tabular}\end{center}
For the other six extremal counts in Theorem~\ref{main}, the same
calculation already gives respectively $20,18,16,14,28,30$ for
$(g,\lambda)=(5,2),(6,4),(6,6),(6,8),(7,4),(8,16)$.
Every integer below a claimed minimum is therefore either excluded
by this acquired bound, excluded by~\eqref{div}, or explicitly tested
in the displayed finite list. A separate exclusion at a larger,
unlisted order is not inferred.

\section{The retained classifications}
We use the complete connected cubic lists in the House of Graphs
meta-directory~\cite{HoG}. The directory explicitly describes its
lists as containing all pairwise nonisomorphic graphs in the stated
class. Its cubic page identifies the minimum-girth classifications:
\texttt{snarkhunter} supplies girths six and seven, with independent
\texttt{genreg}/\texttt{minibaum} confirmations covering our orders;
\texttt{genreg} and \texttt{minibaum} also independently confirm the
girth-eight numbers. The girth-five numbers at our order are
confirmed by all three generators. We retain this classification
assertion, the exact primary table and the actual graph lists.
The girth-nine list is attributed there to McKay, Myrvold and Nadon;
it is used only for a supplementary comparison below.

Here are all sixteen inputs. The middle column is a \emph{minimum}
girth, not a requirement of exact girth. The last column lists every
positive edge-girth-regular result as $(g,\lambda):\text{count}$.
A dash means that no graph in that list passes the test.
