There are $285\,148$ input records. This is a sum of list sizes,
not a count of distinct graphs across all sixteen lists: for example,
the order-twenty minimum-girth-six list is contained in the
minimum-girth-five list. We independently check that each list
contains pairwise nonisomorphic graphs using complete canonical
adjacency certificates from \texttt{pynauty}. Actual certificate
bytes, rather than their hashes, are compared for distinctness.
This check detects duplication; the retained external classification
supplies exhaustiveness.

The stored independent \texttt{geng} executions at orders fourteen
through twenty-two, and the three complete \texttt{snarkhunter}
executions at $(n,g)=(24,6),(20,5),(28,7)$, provide additional
comparisons of whole canonical class sets. Their successful outputs
are retained. An order-twenty-four \texttt{geng} execution reached
its time limit and supplies no conclusion. We do not verify the
entire generator implementations, nor use partial output as a
complete list. The construction methods of Brinkmann and
Goedgebeur~\cite{BG} retain their original attribution.

\section{An exact cycle test}
\begin{lemma}\label{cycle}
For an edge $uv$ in a graph of girth $g$, the girth cycles containing
$uv$ are in bijection with the $u$--$v$ paths of length $g-1$ in
$X-uv$. These paths are shortest paths whenever any exist.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Delete $uv$ from a girth cycle, orienting the remaining path from $u$
to $v$. Conversely, adding $uv$ to a simple path of length $g-1$
gives such a cycle. A shorter path would give a cycle shorter than
$g$. A walk of the same length with a repeated vertex could be
shortened, so every shortest walk under consideration is simple.
The two constructions are inverse; no factor of two is introduced.
\end{proof}

The program first checks the literal \texttt{graph6} encoding,
including its padding, degree three, and connectedness. It computes
the girth by breadth-first trees from every vertex. For each edge
that is not a tree edge, the two paths to the root and that edge
contain a cycle of length at most $d(u)+d(v)+1$. Thus no candidate
length is smaller than the girth. For a root on a shortest cycle,
the two arcs to its opposite vertex or opposite edge are shortest
paths: a shortcut would create a shorter cycle. In the even case
an alternative choice of parent still leaves an edge with distances
$g/2-1,g/2$; in the odd case the opposite edge has distances
$(g-1)/2,(g-1)/2$. Hence the minimum reported length is exactly $g$.

For each undirected edge $uv$, temporarily delete it and perform
breadth-first search from $u$, through depth $g-1$. Initialize the
path count at $u$ to one. For each edge from layer $i$ to layer $i+1$,
add the count at its first endpoint to the second. Induction on
layers shows that these are exactly the numbers of shortest paths.
All contributions to a layer have arrived before that layer is
processed. By Lemma~\ref{cycle}, a missing $v$ at distance $g-1$
gives zero girth cycles; otherwise the count at $v$ is the required
edge multiplicity. The graph passes precisely when all edges give
the same positive integer. This is the same elementary path-count
principle used in the source's algorithms and our accepted F012
algorithm component, specialized here to complete catalogue inputs.

All path counts are unsigned integers with an explicit overflow
check before addition. The program also requires the final count
to be less than one million. These are checked execution conditions,
not assumptions that discard candidates silently: a violation
terminates the run with failure. Every retained run finishes normally,
with every input consumed. A fresh address/undefined-behaviour
sanitizer execution gives byte-identical results for all sixteen
lists. The decoder admits at most sixty-two vertices; all present
inputs lie within that explicit domain.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{main}]
The retained classifications include every connected cubic graph at
each required order and minimum girth. The test just proved decides
its exact girth and whether every edge has multiplicity $\lambda$.
The complete outputs in the preceding table therefore exclude every
order in the admissible-order table. They also give exactly the
stated numbers of extremal classes. The canonical checks exclude
repetition within each input list. Finally, the nine positive
extremal graphs have explicit vertex bijections to the source
authors' recorded examples. Each bijection is verified against all
vertex pairs. This proves the minima, exclusions and counts.
\end{proof}

\section{Certificates and scope}
An independent traversal enumerates simple cycles starting at their
least vertex and chooses one of the two orientations. It agrees
with the filter on $378$ graphs, including all $71$ labelled cubic
graphs of orders four and six, every positive input, and samples
from all sixteen catalogues. Nine malformed inputs test encoding,
degree, connectedness and girth rejection. The retained source maps
are explicit permutations; their provider is not trusted. The exact
source comparison uses the already accepted positive-witness input,
whose origin is the authors' repository at commit
\texttt{41022f4a3ad84e4502f8889c5b64132225411ca2}. This is a later
repository edition than the fixed manuscript; only literal graphs
are acquired from it.

The nonminimal results at orders twenty and forty-two and the three
girth-nine graphs at order sixty are supplementary. In particular,
the fixed source does not assert an exact extremal count at the two
girth-nine parameters, so these outputs earn no further count credit.
The source lower bounds for $(3,8,2)$ and $(3,8,14)$ remain outside
this component: the missing exclusions are not supplied by the
catalogues used here. No infinity assertion in Conjecture~2 follows
from our finite exclusions.

The source classifications, its constructions and the inherited
lower-bound proof remain attributed. The present contribution is
the common catalogue filter, its proof and independently checked
finite application, and the exact routing of all orders needed for
these source entries. The initial C++ filter and route were written
by the root agent; the publication/recovery agent supplied this
proof, canonical audits, independent controls and source maps.
The entire F012 manuscript still has unpaid numerical obligations.
We claim no whole-original recovery, new conjecture answer, Lean
formalization or mathematical compression measurement here.

